  "enhanced_information": "Primary hemochromatosis is a genetic disorder primarily caused by mutations in the HFE gene, leading to increased intestinal iron absorption and subsequent iron overload in the body. It is classified as an autosomal recessive condition, meaning that two copies of the mutated gene (one from each parent) are necessary for the disease to manifest. This condition is not linked to the X chromosome and does not involve mitochondrial inheritance. The consensus supports that primary hemochromatosis is not mitochondrial and is not X-linked, confirming its autosomal recessive nature."
